- How would you handle model drift and monitoring for the SageMaker classifier?
- How would you enforce data residency and access controls across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with Rekognition Video, including asynchronous results and integration with DynamoDB and S3. It also exercises privacy, error handling, and cost trade-offs in a beginner-friendly AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Rekognition Video asynchronous APIs (moderation, labels)
- SNS/SQS notifications and S3 event triggers
- Lambda orchestration and retries
- DynamoDB upsert patterns and compact metadata design
- IAM least privilege and KMS for encryption
- Privacy, retention, and cost considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// process Rekognition output and update DynamoDB
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
async function upsertVideoIndex(videoId, s3Key, labels, status){
  const item = { videoId, s3Key, labelList: labels, moderationStatus: status, timestamp: Date.now() };
  await ddb.put({TableName:'VideoIndex', Item:item}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 1000+ concurrent videos and handle partial failures?
- What changes would you make to support different video formats and longer durations while controlling costs?

## Why This Is Asked
Explores multi-tenant data isolation, real-time latency, and policy governance in production-grade AI pipelines beyond prior questions.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time streaming with Kinesis Data Streams
- SageMaker model versioning and endpoints
- Per-tenant isolation in DynamoDB with TTL and encryption
- PII redaction before archival in S3
- IAM, KMS, VPC endpoints, and cost-monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function score(msg) {
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({
    EndpointName: 'moderation-v1',
    ContentType: 'text/plain',
    Body: msg
  }).promise();
  return res.Body.toString();
}
```

